<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mozambique</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mozambique is a country on the coast of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The Portuguese established trading posts on its coast from the early sixteenth century and pushed inland along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a>, interacting with and undermining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a>. Mozambique remained a Portuguese colony until independence in 1975 after a long war, one of the last parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mozambique.jpg" alt="Mozambique" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FRELIMO · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mozambique.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mozambique lies on the southeast coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, Tanzania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. The Zambezi River divides it into two topographical regions. North of the river a narrow coastal strip gives way to inland hills, low plateaus and rugged highlands covered with miombo woodlands. South of it the lowlands are broader. The climate is tropical, with a wet season from October to March and a dry season from April to September.</p><p>The country was named Moçambique by the Portuguese after the Island of Mozambique. The island's name is derived from Musa ibn Bique, an Arab trader who first visited it, later lived there and was its ruler when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> called in 1498. The island-town was the capital of the Portuguese colony until 1898, when the capital moved south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Lourenço Marques</a>, now Maputo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> peoples migrated into Southeast Africa as early as the 4th century BCE. Between the 1st and 5th centuries CE waves of migration passed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> valley into the plateau and coastal areas. They established agricultural communities and herding societies and brought the technology of iron smelting and smithing. From the late first millennium CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> networks reached as far south as the port town of Chibuene. From the 9th century, port towns grew along the East African coast and shared in an incipient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was often adopted by urban elites, which facilitated trade.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>, Angoche and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique-island">Mozambique Island</a> were regional powers by the 15th century. Inland states such as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a> supplied gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, which were exchanged up the coast to larger ports such as Kilwa and Mombasa. Vasco da Gama's voyage of 1498 marked the Portuguese entry into the region. They gained control of the Island of Mozambique and of Sofala in the early 16th century, and by the 1530s traders and prospectors seeking gold had set up garrisons and trading posts at Sena and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tete">Tete</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a>. Portuguese posts and forts displaced Arab commercial and military dominance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mozambique is the Republic of Mozambique, a presidential republic with its capital and largest city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Maputo</a>. It became independent in 1975 after more than four centuries of Portuguese rule, and a civil war followed from 1977 to 1992. Portuguese is the only official language, alongside local languages such as Tsonga, Makhuwa and Sena. The Makonde are known for their wood carving and masks, and the Chopi for their marimba music and dance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mozambique” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and short of supplies after forces from the Belgian Congo had attacked from the west, in the Battle of Tabora, Lettow-Vorbeck abandoned the colony, withdrew south into Portuguese <b>Mozambique</b> and then into Northern Rhodesia, and agreed to a ceasefire on hearing of the armistice concluded three days earlier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese Empire that grew from these voyages lasted until 1999, when Macau was handed to China, and left Portuguese as the language of Brazil, Angola, <b>Mozambique</b> and other countries, today spoken by more than 250 million people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hundreds of stone-walled sites in the same style, among them Khami, the capital of the Kingdom of Butua, and Manyikeni in <b>Mozambique</b>, continued the forms developed there, and traditions trace the founder of the Mutapa state to its royal family.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Where Malindi chose alliance, Mombasa repeatedly refused to submit, and the Portuguese came to see its harbour as the key to controlling the coast north of <b>Mozambique</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mutapa Empire, also known as the state of the Mwene Mutapa, arose in the mid-fifteenth century in the region between the Zambezi and Limpopo rivers in present-day Zimbabwe and <b>Mozambique</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An Order-in-Council of 9 May 1891 placed the region under company administration, and its borders with <b>Mozambique</b>, Angola, German territory and the Congo Free State were fixed by treaties between 1890 and 1905; a Belgian expedition's agreement with Msiri in 1891 kept Katanga for the Congo Free State…</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
